<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/reaction-kinetics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Thermodynamics says whether a reaction <em>can</em> go; kinetics says <strong>how fast it
    actually goes</strong> — and they disagree constantly. Petrol + oxygen is thermodynamically
    thrilled and kinetically bored at room temperature (no spark, no fire). Kinetics is the
    study of the in-between: collision energy, molecular orientation, and the activation hill.
    It is also, quietly, the chemistry of everyday engineering — food storage, catalytic
    converters, drug dosing, explosive safety — because civilisation spends much of its time
    either speeding reactions up or slowing them down.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Arrhenius in action — heat the system and the rate constant climbs exponentially, steeply for hard reactions" widget="plotter" config={arrheniusCfg} />

  <p>
    Arrhenius's law (1889): k = A·e^(−Ea/kT). The readout slider is temperature — drag it and
    watch rates multiply, not add. The kitchen rule of thumb holds: roughly 10 °C warmer
    <strong>doubles</strong> most reaction rates, which is why fridges preserve food and fever
    burns through your metabolic reserves.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring rate: graphs before guesses</h2>
  <p>
    Rate is change in amount per unit time — and since rate falls as reactants are consumed,
    the honest measurement is the <strong>gradient of a concentration–time graph</strong>
    (steepest at the start). The experimenter's toolkit picks whatever the reaction leaks:
    gas volume (magnesium in hydrochloric acid, syringe in a gas syringe), mass loss (a
    carbonate fizzing on a balance), colour change (colorimetry on the thiosulfate "disappearing
    cross"), or quench-and-titrate (freeze aliquots by dilution/cooling, then titrate — the
    classical method). Worked numbers: 0.12 g of magnesium (0.005 mol) in excess acid gives
    0.005 mol of H₂ — about 120 cm³ at room temperature; collecting 60 cm³ in the first
    40 seconds gives an average early rate of 1.5 cm³/s, while the next 60 cm³ crawls in over
    5 minutes. The slowdown is the whole theory in one graph: as acid is consumed, fewer
    collisions per second. To get an <em>instantaneous</em> rate you draw the tangent at a
    point — steepest at t = 0 (the "initial rate", the cleanest number for comparing
    experiments, since nothing has yet been consumed).
  </p>
  <p>
    Two quick quantitative confirmations of the collision picture. <strong>Surface area:</strong>
    one marble of chalk (calcium carbonate, 1 cm³) exposes about 6 cm²; the same volume as
    1 µm powder exposes roughly 60,000 cm² — ten thousandfold more reactive frontier, which
    is why the powder fizzes furiously while the chip idles. <strong>Concentration:</strong>
    the iodine-clock reaction (persulfate with iodide, or the bisulfite classic) is set up
    so a sharp blue-black appears after a delay; doubling one reagent's concentration halves
    the delay — direct, visible evidence that rate scales with collision frequency. Clock
    reactions exist because chemists wanted rate made <em>timable</em>: the stop-watch is
    the readout.
  </p>

  <h2>Collision theory: the four dials</h2>
  <p>
    Reactions happen when particles collide with <strong>enough energy (≥ the activation
    energy Ea)</strong> <em>and</em> the <strong>right orientation</strong>. Every rate
    factor is one of four dials on that statement:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Concentration/pressure</strong> — more particles per volume, more collisions
    per second. Acid at 2 mol/L fizzes magnesium roughly twice as fast as 1 mol/L.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface area</strong> — solids only react at their surface. Powder reacts
    faster than chips (flour mills and sawmills explode: airborne dust is enormous surface
    area; the classic demonstration is a lidded-tin "flour bomb", and the real ones are
    grain-elevator disasters).</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature</strong> — particles move faster, colliding harder <em>and</em>
    more often; but the big effect is the tail of fast movers clearing Ea (below).</li>
    <li><strong>Catalyst</strong> — a different route with a lower Ea, without being
    consumed: it holds reactants in reactive orientations or pays part of the bond-breaking
    cost in stages. More collisions clear the (now lower) bar.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> heating "only makes particles move faster, so collisions
  are more frequent" — as if the modest speed increase explained the doubled rate. Collision
  frequency rises only slightly (by √T); the violence of the effect comes from the
  <strong>energy distribution's tail</strong>: a 10 °C rise roughly doubles the fraction of
  molecules carrying enough energy to react. It is the exponential statistics, not the
  speedometer, that runs the world.</p>

  <h2>Temperature: Arrhenius's exponential</h2>
  <p>
    Svante Arrhenius gave the dependence its form: <strong>k = A·e^(−Ea/RT)</strong> — the
    fraction of collisions with sufficient energy falls exponentially with Ea/RT, so rate
    constants multiply when temperature climbs. Worked check with real numbers: for Ea =
    50 kJ/mol (typical), warming from 25 °C to 35 °C changes the exponent factor by
    e^(Ea/R·(1/298 − 1/308)) ≈ e^0.65 ≈ <strong>1.9</strong> — the "doubling per 10 °C" rule,
    derived, not remembered. Run it further and the multiplication compounds: 40 °C of
    warming is not 8× but about 2⁴ ≈ 16×. The widget shows the flip side: hard reactions
    (high Ea, the steep curve) are <em>more</em> temperature-sensitive — cold kills them
    first, which is exactly why refrigeration works and why enzymes (whose Ea is
    enzymically lowered) still slow dramatically in a fridge. The same exponential is the
    reason freezer burn, spoilage clocks and expiry dates exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Rate laws: orders and half-lives</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>rate law</strong> expresses the measured dependence: rate = k[A]^m[B]^n, with
    the orders m and n found <em>only</em> by experiment — <strong>they are not the
    stoichiometric coefficients</strong>. H₂ + Br₂ → HBr looks like a clean 1:1 reaction and
    has a rate law so awkward it took chemists years to untangle (a chain mechanism hides
    behind the equation). Doubling a reactant that doubles the rate means first order;
    quadrupling the rate means second order; no effect means zero order. First-order
    reactions carry a gift: a <strong>constant half-life</strong> — 50% gone after one
    half-life, 25% after two, 12.5% after three — the mathematics behind carbon-14 dating,
    drug clearance (a medicine with a 6-hour half-life is "gone" after about 30 hours) and
    every exponential decay. For multi-step reactions, the <strong>rate-determining
    step</strong> — the slowest elementary step, the molecular traffic jam — sets the
    overall rate, which is how mechanisms are diagnosed: propose steps, predict the rate
    law, compare with experiment.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked diagnosis. Suppose reaction A + B is studied by the initial-rates method, and
    the data read: doubling [A] (holding [B] fixed) doubles the rate; doubling [B] (holding
    [A] fixed) quadruples the rate. First order in A, second order in B, so
    rate = k[A][B]². Feed one trial's numbers to find k (with units — mol⁻²·L²·s⁻¹ for a
    third-order law, and examiners check them), then <em>test</em> the law by predicting a
    fourth experiment. The method is measurement-first empiricism: the orders describe the
    mechanism's slowest step, not the reaction's formula, and they are discovered exactly
    the way this paragraph discovers them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution</strong> earns a second mention here because
    kinetics is where it pays out. Plot molecular energies and you get the familiar
    long-tailed curve; shade the portion beyond Ea and that shaded area is the fraction of
    collisions that can react. Everything in this chapter is that one picture under
    different operations: heating stretches the tail (exponentially more area beyond Ea);
    a catalyst drags the Ea line leftward into the curve's bulk; concentrating the gas
    simply rescales the whole curve. Students who can sketch this diagram and perform one
    shaded-area argument on it can answer essentially every kinetics question a course will
    ask.
  </p>

  <h2>Catalysts: from the factory to the cell</h2>
  <p>
    Catalysts are the leverage points of chemistry, and one clarification first: they are
    <strong>not consumed</strong>. They participate — temporarily bonding, bending, handing
    electrons back and forth — and are regenerated, ready for the next cycle; a catalytic
    converter's platinum survives a decade of exhausts. (They do eventually "die", but from
    poisoning — leaded petrol famously gummed converter surfaces — or clogging, not from
    being used up.) <strong>Heterogeneous</strong> ones
    (solid surfaces) anchor the Haber process (iron, feeding billions), hydrogenation of
    fats (nickel), and the <strong>catalytic converter</strong> — platinum, palladium and
    rhodium honeycombs that let CO and unburnt hydrocarbons find oxygen at exhaust
    temperatures, cutting those pollutants by over 90% since the 1970s. <strong>Enzymes</strong>
    are biology's catalysts, and their performance defies industrial chemistry: by cradling
    substrates in precisely the reactive orientation (the "lock-and-key" idea, refined into
    induced fit), they accelerate reactions by factors of 10⁶ to beyond 10¹⁷ — carbonic
    anhydrase hydrates about a million CO₂ molecules per second per enzyme. Without that
    speed-up, the reactions of life would take geological timescales at body temperature;
    enzyme kinetics (Michaelis–Menten, 1913 — rates that saturate when the enzyme is
    busy) is where chemistry and medicine meet. Poisons often work by <strong>inhibiting
    enzymes</strong>: carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin, cyanide jams a respiration enzyme —
    toxicology is applied kinetics. And remember the equilibrium chapter's rule: catalysts
    buy time, never yield — no catalyst in history has ever shifted an equilibrium position,
    however hard industrial chemists have wished otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>One radical, 100,000 molecules: catalysis at planetary scale.</strong> The
    stratospheric ozone layer is destroyed by chlorine radicals released from CFCs: one Cl
    atom catalyses the conversion of roughly 100,000 ozone molecules before it is
    scavenged. That multiplication — a catalyst's defining feature — is why a few parts per
    trillion of CFCs opened the Antarctic ozone hole, and why the Montreal Protocol (1987),
    by banning the <em>source</em>, is the most successful environmental treaty in history.
    Kinetics, at altitude, saved the sky.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>From hill to hobbyhorse: measuring Ea.</strong> Run a reaction at several
    temperatures, plot ln k against 1/T, and the Arrhenius equation straightens into a line
    whose slope is −Ea/R — the standard lab for activation energies, and a beautiful
    example of a curved law made linear by choosing the right axes (the same trick the
    exponentials chapter used on Moore's law). Typical Ea values: 10–30 kJ/mol for
    enzyme-catalysed steps, 50–100 kJ/mol for uncatalysed solution chemistry, 150+ kJ/mol
    for the stubborn ones (nitrogen's triple bond head-on: ~500 kJ/mol to split unaided —
    which is why nitrogen fertiliser needed a catalyst and a century of effort).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Explosions and runaways.</strong> Exothermic reactions heat themselves; heating
    accelerates them; acceleration makes more heat — positive feedback until cooling
    catches up or the vessel leaves. Thermal runaway is why oil refineries watch
    temperature alarms more than pressure gauges, why compost heaps spontaneously smoulder,
    and why the 1947 Texas City disaster (a shipload of ammonium nitrate fertiliser) is
    required reading for process engineers. The mathematics of when runaway ignites
    (Frank-Kamenetskii theory) is kinetics' practical summit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier: watching bonds break.</strong> Ahmed Zewail's femtochemistry
    (Nobel 1999) fires laser pulses tens of femtoseconds long — 10⁻¹⁵ s, the time atoms take
    to move one bond-length — and films transition states directly. We can now watch the
    activation hill being climbed, frame by frame, and test mechanism hypotheses against
    movies rather than rates. Open questions crowd the field: how enzymes couple their
    motions to chemistry (dynamics beyond the lock-and-key picture), how to design
    catalysts for nitrogen fixation under mild conditions (the "holy grail" that would
    retire the Haber plant), and how combustion's hundreds of simultaneous elementary
    reactions can be tamed for carbon-neutral fuels. Kinetics began by asking how fast;
    it now asks how, in as much detail as cameras allow.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Arrhenius (1889); Michaelis & Menten (1913); Atkins & de Paula, Physical Chemistry (Kinetics); Zewail, femtochemistry reviews (Nature, 2000); WMO ozone assessment reports.</p>
</TopicPage>
